linux-redmi-marble (7.2.8+marble7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add debian/patches/iris-decode-order-output.patch: expose the standard
    decoder display-delay controls on the HFI gen2 decoder capability table
    and forward HFI_PROP_DECODE_ORDER_OUTPUT when userspace asks for it.
    Without it the VA-API backend (libva-v4l2, built as its own package)
    aborts in its decoder constructor with "S_CTRL DISPLAY_DELAY (Iris
    decode-order patch required): Invalid argument" and every VA-API
    application silently falls back to software decoding. marble reaches
    that table through sm8450_data -> iris_vpu20_4v_gen2_desc ->
    iris_hfi_gen2_vpu2_data, i.e. the sm8550 decoder caps, not the gen1 ones.
  * debian/rules now applies debian/patches/series in its configure target.
    debian/source/format is 3.0 (native), so dpkg-source never applies the
    series: doing it in the package instead of in the workflow keeps a local
    dpkg-buildpackage run identical to CI and makes a patch that stops
    applying fail the build loudly instead of shipping an unpatched kernel
    (the failure mode that already bit the ath11k MAC patch).
  * Verify step: the built qcom-iris module must carry the new decode-order
    handler, next to the existing ath11k wlan_mac.bin assertion.

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:30:00 +0800

linux-redmi-marble (7.2.8+marble6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * debian/rules: skip dh_auto_clean. Upstream's MRPROPER_FILES lists the
    top-level debian/ directory (make deb-pkg creates it there, so
    'make distclean' deletes it) - which is exactly where this packaging
    lives. dh_auto_clean therefore wiped debian/control and dh_clean
    aborted the clean target before the build even started. The tree
    arrives as a fresh tarball, so there is nothing to clean.
  * The CI workflow now applies the ath11k WLAN MAC patch explicitly.
    debian/source/format is 3.0 (native), so dpkg-source ignores
    debian/patches and the built kernel came up without it - a random MAC
    every boot, which is what the patch and marble-mac-restore.service
    exist to prevent. CI asserts on the wlan_mac.bin string in the built
    ath11k module, the same way the local build did.

